## The Specification Pattern – A Practical Guide in Java

The **Specification** pattern is a powerful way to encapsulate *business rules* (also called *predicates* or *criteria*) so that they can be reused, combined, and tested independently of the rest of your code.  

It is especially useful when:

* You have many small rules that need to be applied in different combinations.
* You want to keep domain logic out of data‑access or UI code.
* You need to express the same rule in different contexts (in‑memory filtering, SQL, REST query, etc.).

Below is a full walkthrough:

| Topic | What you’ll learn |
|-------|-------------------|
| 1️⃣ What the pattern is | Why we use it |
| 2️⃣ Core interface | `Specification<T>` |
| 3️⃣ Combinator defaults | `and()`, `or()`, `not()` |
| 4️⃣ Concrete specs | Simple & complex examples |
| 5️⃣ Repository integration | Passing specs to a DAO |
| 6️⃣ Demo code | Java 17+ style |
| 7️⃣ Unit test | Verify correctness |
| 8️⃣ Pros & cons | When to use or avoid |

---

### 1️⃣ What is a Specification?

A *Specification* is a predicate that checks whether an object satisfies a certain condition.  
Formally:

```
Specification<T> = T → Boolean
```

In OO terms it is a *value object* that can be:

* Evaluated (`isSatisfiedBy`).
* Combined with other specifications (`and`, `or`, `not`).
* Translated into other query languages (SQL, JPA Criteria, etc.).

---

### 2️⃣ Core Interface

```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface Specification<T> {

    /**
     * Returns true if the supplied candidate satisfies the specification.
     */
    boolean isSatisfiedBy(T candidate);

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
       Combinator helpers – default methods so every spec gets them
       ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

    default Specification<T> and(Specification<T> other) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(other);
        return candidate -> this.isSatisfiedBy(candidate) && other.isSatisfiedBy(candidate);
    }

    default Specification<T> or(Specification<T> other) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(other);
        return candidate -> this.isSatisfiedBy(candidate) || other.isSatisfiedBy(candidate);
    }

    default Specification<T> not() {
        return candidate -> !this.isSatisfiedBy(candidate);
    }
}
```

*Why `@FunctionalInterface`?*  
You can create specs as lambda expressions or method references.

---

### 3️⃣ Combinator Defaults

The default methods give every specification a *small DSL*:

```java
Specification<Product> cheap  = new PriceLessThanSpecification(100);
Specification<Product> books = new CategorySpecification("Books");
Specification<Product> cheapBooks = cheap.and(books);
Specification<Product> notCheapBooks = cheapBooks.not();
```

The resulting objects are still `Specification<Product>`; they can be passed around just like any other spec.

---

### 4️⃣ Concrete Specifications

#### 4.1 Simple examples

```java
public record Product(String name, double price, String category) { }

// price < threshold
public final class PriceLessThanSpecification implements Specification<Product> {
    private final double threshold;
    public PriceLessThanSpecification(double threshold) { this.threshold = threshold; }
    @Override public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Product p) { return p.price() < threshold; }
}

// category equals value
public final class CategorySpecification implements Specification<Product> {
    private final String category;
    public CategorySpecification(String category) { this.category = category; }
    @Override public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Product p) { return p.category().equalsIgnoreCase(category); }
}
```

#### 4.2 Composite example – “discounted books”

```java
public final class DiscountedBooksSpecification implements Specification<Product> {
    private final Specification<Product> base = new CategorySpecification("Books")
                                                   .and(new PriceLessThanSpecification(50));
    @Override public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Product p) { return base.isSatisfiedBy(p); }
}
```

You could also use the DSL directly:

```java
Specification<Product> discountedBooks = new CategorySpecification("Books")
                                            .and(new PriceLessThanSpecification(50));
```

---

### 5️⃣ Repository Integration

Often a specification is passed to a repository (DAO) to fetch entities that match it.

```java
public interface ProductRepository {
    List<Product> findAll(Specification<Product> spec);
}
```

**In‑memory implementation (demo only)**

```java
public class InMemoryProductRepository implements ProductRepository {
    private final List<Product> data;

    public InMemoryProductRepository(List<Product> data) { this.data = List.copyOf(data); }

    @Override public List<Product> findAll(Specification<Product> spec) {
        return data.stream()
                   .filter(spec::isSatisfiedBy)
                   .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

> **Real‑world note:**  
> With JPA/Hibernate you’d translate a `Specification<Product>` into a `Predicate` or `CriteriaQuery`. Many frameworks (Spring Data JPA, Hibernate, MyBatis) already provide a `Specification` abstraction.

---

### 6️⃣ Demo Code – Putting It All Together

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class SpecDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Product> products = List.of(
            new Product("Effective Java", 45.0, "Books"),
            new Product("Laptop", 1200.0, "Electronics"),
            new Product("Java Mug", 12.0, "Merch"),
            new Product("Headphones", 89.0, "Electronics"),
            new Product("Java for Beginners", 35.0, "Books")
        );

        ProductRepository repo = new InMemoryProductRepository(products);

        Specification<Product> cheapBooks =
                new CategorySpecification("Books")
                .and(new PriceLessThanSpecification(50));

        List<Product> result = repo.findAll(cheapBooks);

        result.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Product[name=Effective Java, price=45.0, category=Books]
Product[name=Java for Beginners, price=35.0, category=Books]
```

