### 7️⃣ Unit Test (JUnit 5)

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.*;
import java.util.*;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class SpecificationTest {

    private static final List<Product> PRODUCTS = List.of(
        new Product("A", 10.0, "Books"),
        new Product("B", 60.0, "Books"),
        new Product("C", 40.0, "Electronics")
    );

    @Test
    void cheapBooksSpecShouldFilterCorrectly() {
        Specification<Product> spec = new CategorySpecification("Books")
                                     .and(new PriceLessThanSpecification(50));

        List<Product> matched = new InMemoryProductRepository(PRODUCTS)
                                .findAll(spec);

        assertEquals(1, matched.size());
        assertEquals("A", matched.get(0).name());
    }

    @Test
    void notSpecWorks() {
        Specification<Product> cheap = new PriceLessThanSpecification(50);
        Specification<Product> notCheap = cheap.not();

        List<Product> result = new InMemoryProductRepository(PRODUCTS)
                               .findAll(notCheap);

        assertEquals(2, result.size());
        assertTrue(result.stream().anyMatch(p -> p.name().equals("B")));
    }
}
```

---

### 8️⃣ Pros & Cons

| ✔️ Pros | ❌ Cons |
|---------|---------|
| **Reusability** – One rule can be used in many places. | **Verbosity** – A lot of small classes if you don't use lambdas. |
| **Testability** – Specs are pure functions; easy to unit‑test. | **Learning curve** – Teams unfamiliar with functional style may struggle. |
| **Composition** – Combine rules without new conditional logic. | **Performance** – In‑memory filtering is simple, but for DB queries you need translation logic. |
| **Domain‑Driven Design** – Keeps domain rules in domain layer. | **Debugging** – Complex composed specs can be hard to trace. |
| **Readability** – DSL (`and`, `or`, `not`) reads like English. | – |

---

## Bottom‑Line

*The Specification pattern is nothing more than a clean, composable way to represent “is this entity valid?” checks.*  
With Java’s functional features (lambdas, method references, default methods), the pattern becomes almost trivial to implement while still keeping the codebase clean and testable.

Feel free to adapt the code to your own domain: replace `Product` with `Order`, `Customer`, `Invoice`, etc. The same principles apply!
